Supersedes the VIS-04 patch that failed `tsc` at ship step 4. The defect was
mine and the guard was right; the fix is in the last section.

THE REPORT WAS ACCURATE ABOUT THE EXPERIENCE AND WRONG ABOUT THE CAUSE

Three screenshots from a phone: "How do you want the room to feel?", then "We
found your room", then a photograph of grey carpet with four corner handles on
it and no new floor anywhere.

I reproduced that exact input — a portrait photograph of one flat carpet
surface — through the real pipeline:

    confidence : weak
    quad       : the default, bottom 45% with a taper
    painted    : 1.000
    pixels changed: 110,566 of 307,200 = 36.0%

The renderer worked. It changed a third of the picture. Nobody saw it, because
the verify screen paints `beforeRef` — the visitor's own photograph, unchanged,
with the corners over it — and the composite appears two taps later, behind a
screen asking how they want the room to feel.

Upload a photo of your room → see your room → answer a question about feelings
→ see your room → then see a floor. The one moment this feature exists for was
third, and the person reporting it gave up at the second.

WHAT CHANGES

  · THE VERIFY SCREEN PAINTS THE COMPOSITE. A real Ecowoods floor is in the
    room the instant the analysis finishes, and the four corners sit on top of
    it — correction and payoff on one screen, and dragging a corner visibly
    moves the new floor.
  · THE PRIMARY ACTION GOES STRAIGHT TO THE STUDIO. "Show me every floor in
    this room." The feel question is still there, second, as "Help me choose":
    it is a better question once somebody has seen one floor in their room than
    before they have seen any.
  · `stage` IS A DEPENDENCY of the repaint effect. Verify and studio are
    different elements, so the transition hands afterRef a NEW, blank canvas.
    Without it the visitor arrives at the studio looking at an empty box — the
    same bug again, one screen further on.

SIG-01 · ECOWOODS.CA, IN THE PIXELS

The most-shared artefact this feature will ever produce is a screenshot, and a
screenshot carries no DOM. A mark in the HTML around the canvas is a mark that
is not in the thing that gets sent to a partner, a group chat or a contractor.

lib/floor-studio/signature.ts draws the mark, the floor's catalogue name, the
estimated range and ecowoods.ca into the bottom of every rendered surface — the
verify screen, the studio, the live camera — and into the downloaded PNG, via a
new overlay hook on pixelsToDataUrl. The host comes from SITE_URL and the range
from the estimate that produced the picture, so a signature can never name a
domain nobody owns or a price nobody published. LiveRoom's own copy of that
drawing code is deleted; there is one now.

TEX-01 · REAL PHOTOGRAPHIC WOOD — BUILT, TESTED, AND NOT TURNED ON

scripts/textures/build-grain.py cuts one seamless tile per species from
apps/web/public/gallery/<species>-wideplank-02-detail.webp — photographs of the
actual product. It divides out the photograph's own illumination, because
otherwise its shadow is pasted into somebody else's room AND the renderer
multiplies their light in on top of it, applying it twice. It tiles without a
mirror, because reflecting a diagonal makes a chevron and wood grain has no
symmetry. The renderer samples it in board-local INCHES — so one texture serves
straight, diagonal, herringbone and chevron alike — with bilinear filtering, a
per-board offset so no two boards repeat, and a mip pyramid whose level comes
from the homography's own projective divisor, which is literally the depth.

IT IS NOT WIRED TO THE INTERFACE, BECAUSE MEASURED SIDE BY SIDE IT DOES NOT WIN
YET. The colour is right and the hickory character marks are real; in the far
half of the frame it still reads noisier than the procedural grain it was meant
to replace, because a 512-pixel tile minified across a receding plane aliases
and the mip selection does not fully answer that. It needs anisotropic sampling
along the board's own axis, which is a patch and not a paragraph. Four crop
strategies were tried and three picked a board seam, which then repeats INSIDE
every board — white oak came out as vertical stripes, visibly worse than what
ships. The crops are named by hand now, each with its reason.

Shipping it in this state would make the product worse, which is the one thing
this repository is not allowed to do.

WHAT BROKE THE FIRST ATTEMPT, AND WHAT STOPS IT RECURRING

    error TS2448: Block-scoped variable 'estimate' used before its declaration.

The paint effect named `estimate` in its dependency array thirty-two lines
above the useMemo that declares it — a temporal dead zone. It is invisible in
review because the name is right there and reads fine, and I have no tsc in the
environment I write these patches in, which is exactly why ship.sh runs one.

The effect moved below `estimate` rather than reaching it through a ref: a ref
would have hidden the ordering instead of correcting it. Then I wrote the check
I should have had — every identifier in a hook dependency array must be
declared above it, counting brace depth so a `const` inside a callback does not
count as a shadow — and ran it across every component in apps/web/app. One
false positive from the naive version (BookingScheduler's local `data`), zero
real findings after the depth fix.

Verified: 66 guards pass. 65,495 assertions across the floor-studio, navigation
and assistant suites, 0 failed — 63,530 of them the existing renderer, which
the texture path and the signature leave untouched.

